  "name": null,
  "problem": "Another particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$",
  "worked_solution": "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$",
